Review Admission Controller

	ps -ef | grep kube-apiserver
		check --enable-admission-plugin parameters for PodSecurity

	check each namespace for policies allowing
		privileged
 		allowPrivilegeEscalation
 		runAsRoot (or no runAsUser/runAsGroup/runAsNonRoot)
		Added Capabilities
			NET_RAW, SYS_ADMIN, etc
		hostPath volumes
		hostPorts
		ReadOnlyRootFilesystem
		spec.hostIPC
		spec.hostNetwork
		image provenance
		securityContext.windowsOptions.hostProcess (Windows containers only)


	Review validatingwebhookconfigurations and mutatingwebhookconfigurations
		k get validatingwebhookconfigurations
		k get validatingwebhookconfigurations <name> -o yaml
		k get mutatingwebhookconfigurations
		k get mutatingwebhookconfigurations <name> -o yaml

		Check caBundle:

			Red flags:
				Missing caBundle
				Empty or null bundle
				Extremely short cert expirations (misconfigured operator)
				WebhookService with no proper TLS secret
	
		Check API groups and resources it intercepts:

			Wide-scope webhooks are a security concern unless they’re from a trusted system (e.g., Gatekeeper, Kyverno).
			
			rules:
			- apiGroups: ["*"]
			  resources: ["*"]
			  operations: ["CREATE","UPDATE","DELETE"]

		Check failurePolicy:

			Fail	
				Reject request if webhook is down	
				Secure (prevents bypass)
			Ignore	
				Allow request if webhook is down	
				High risk (bypassable)
		
			FailAfterDryRun	
				Newer option; safer than Ignore	
				Medium risk

		Check sideEffects:
			Should be: "sideEffects: None"
			If it's "Unknown" or missing → possible API instability.

		Check admissionReviewVersions:
			Should include "v1".
			If only "v1beta1" → indicates outdated / potentially vulnerable webhook.


API Server Audit Logs
	ps -ef | grep kube-apiserver

	Ensure --audit-log-path is set
	Ensure some reasonable combination of --audit-log-maxage (default 0, never deleted), --audit-log-maxbackup (default 0, old log deleted when maxsize reached), and/or --audit-log-maxsize (default 100MB) are set to retain logs for DFIR


